<section class="w-full max-w-4xl mx-auto py-12" id="problem-framing">
  <h2
    class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-2"
  >
    Why measure against human data
  </h2>
  <p class="text-muted mb-4 max-w-2xl">
    Critiques of cross-provider homogenization need a ground truth to measure
    against. Real human response distributions provide one.
  </p>

  <div
    class="rounded-lg border border-muted/20 bg-surface p-6 mb-6 dark:bg-surface-dark"
  >
    <p class="text-sm text-text/80 dark:text-text-dark/80 mb-3">
      Romasanta, Thomas, and Levina
      (<a
        href="https://hbr.org/2026/03/researchers-asked-llms-for-strategic-advice-they-got-trendslop-in-return"
        class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">HBR, March 2026</a
      >) ran 15,000 strategic-advice queries across seven frontier LLMs and
      found that responses converged on the same trend-aligned recommendations
      regardless of scenario context. They called this <em>trendslop</em>: a
      shared attractor across providers, not a quirk of any single model. The
      critique is sound, but it has no measurement behind it. Cross-model
      consensus is only evidence of failure if you can point to the
      distribution the models should have matched, and open-ended strategic
      questions have no such ground truth.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm text-text/80 dark:text-text-dark/80">
      SynthBench supplies that measurement. Every question we evaluate has a
      human response distribution from Pew American Trends Panel, SubPOP
      subpopulations, or GlobalOpinionQA cross-country data. We score how far
      each provider's output is from that distribution, overall and for each
      demographic slice.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3
    class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-text dark:text-text-dark mb-3"
  >
    Two separate diagnostics
  </h3>
  <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 mb-6">
    <div
      class="rounded-lg border border-muted/20 bg-surface p-5 dark:bg-surface-dark"
    >
      <h4 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Ground-truth fidelity</h4>
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted mb-2">
        Model vs. human distribution
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text/80 dark:text-text-dark/80">
        Mean Jensen-Shannon divergence against real survey responses, rolled up
        into the Survey Parity Score (SPS). This is SynthBench's primary
        metric. The sections below describe how it is measured.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="rounded-lg border border-muted/20 bg-surface p-5 dark:bg-surface-dark"
    >
      <h4 class="text-base font-semibold mb-1">Cross-provider concordance</h4>
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-muted mb-2">
        Model vs. model distribution
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-text/80 dark:text-text-dark/80">
        Pairwise JSD between raw-LLM providers on the same items. Low
        cross-provider JSD with high human-alignment means real population
        variance is being tracked; low cross-provider JSD with low
        human-alignment is the trendslop signature.{" "}
        <a
          href="/findings#cross-provider-jsd"
          class="text-primary dark:text-primary-light underline underline-offset-2 hover:no-underline"
          >See the cross-provider JSD matrix →</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="text-sm text-muted max-w-2xl">
    The rest of this page documents the metrics, datasets, baselines, scoring
    protocol, cost model, and related literature behind those two diagnostics.
  </p>
</section>
